;;; A rule whose `:match` is `()` never fired — **W31**, register row
;;; `W31/empty-match-false` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact and a rule with no premise that concludes `(false)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An empty conjunction is true, so the rule fires once (Q-M1e2.36): root is
;;; dead and R is empty. Its core is `[]`, the empty set (Q-M1e2.27), and
;;; `refuted by` names `r`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The rule loaded and never fired (`ein saturate FILE --dump`): root was
;;; alive, ∅ in R and R two states, ∅ and `{(p a)}`, exit 0 (`ein solve
;;; FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s,
;;; release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `:match ()` is the empty conjunction, so `r` fires once at root and
;;; concludes `(false)`: root is dead and R is empty, root's core `[]`,
;;; printed `∅` (Q-M1f.48), and `refuted by` `r`, the answer above by hand.
;;; The register's `now` is that answer; the section above is the record of
;;; the one it replaced.
;;;
;;;

(relation q Node :why "q holds of {?1}") (relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}")
(q a)
(rule r () :match () :assert (false) :why "the rule has no premise and concludes false, so no state can be")
